## 9. Mathematical Foundations & Physics of HNSW Graphs

### 9.1 Skip-List Hierarchy & Voronoi Partitions
Modern vector search engines (such as Azure AI Search and Qdrant) utilize **Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW)** graphs to achieve sub-10ms Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search across millions of dense vectors.

HNSW constructs a multi-layer graph inspired by probabilistic skip-lists:
* **Layer 0 (Ground Layer)**: Contains 100% of all indexed vectors connected in a high-density proximity graph.
* **Higher Layers ($l = 1, 2, \dots, L_{\text{max}}$)**: Contain exponentially fewer vectors with longer-range links, enabling rapid spatial traversal across high-dimensional manifolds.

The probability that a vector is assigned to layer $l$ follows an exponential decay distribution:

$$P(l) = \exp\left(-\frac{l}{m_L}\right), \quad m_L = \frac{1}{\ln(M)}$$

Where $M$ is the maximum number of bidirectional connections per node.
